          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-disclaimer-3",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The advice offered below is based on the current "
            },
            {
              "type": "link",
              "text": "State of the 2014 DnD 5e Character Optimization Meta",
              "href": "https://rpgbot.net/dnd5/characters/state-of-the-meta/"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " as of when the article was last updated. Keep in mind that the state of the meta periodically changes as new source materials are released, and the article will be updated accordingly as time allows."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-disclaimer-4",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "We will not include 3rd-party content, Unearthed Arcnaa content, or homebrew in guides for official content because we can’t assume that your game will allow them."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-tortle-versions",
      "heading": "Tortle Versions",
      "sourceAnchor": "tortle-versions",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-tortle-versions-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "There are effectively three versions of the Tortle. The original was published in The Tortle Package and then reprinted in Explorer’s Guide to Wildemount."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-tortle-versions-2",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Tasha’s Cauldron of Everything introduced the option Customizing Your Origin rules, which gave us the second version and allowing players to reassign the Tortle’s ability score increases and their skill proficiency."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-tortle-versions-3",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Most recently, Mordenkainen’s Monsters of the Multiverse re-published the Tortle. The changes here were minor, replacing the Tortle’s Ability Score Increase trait with the new standard for ability scores, allowing players to assign either +2/+1 increases or three +1 increases. The update also increased the Tortle’s Claw damage to 1d6 and changes the Tortle’s original fixed skill proficiency, instead allowing you to choose from a small list of options. The list of options is more restrictive than the custom origin rules simply because it’s not the full list of skills, but the options are numerous and good enough that nearly every character will find a good option. You can also choose to be either medium or small."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-tortles-in-the-2024-dnd-rules",
      "heading": "Tortles in the 2024 DnD Rules",
      "sourceAnchor": "tortles-in-the-2024-dnd-rules",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-tortles-in-the-2024-dnd-rules-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Tortles were most recently updated in Monsters of the Multiverse, and have not yet been updated for the 2024 DnD rules. To play a Tortle in the 2024 DnD rules, ignore the Ability Score Increases described in Monsters of the Multiverse. Instead, use the Ability Score Increases provided by your Background in the 2024 rules."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Tortle Classes (Customizable Origins and MMoM)",
      "sourceAnchor": "classes-custom-origin",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classes-custom-origin-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "This section assumes that you’re using the option updated versions of the race, including the “"
            },
            {
              "type": "link",
              "text": "Customizing Your Origin",
              "href": "https://www.dndbeyond.com/sources/tcoe/character-options#CustomizingYourOrigin"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "” rules presented in Tasha’s Cauldron of Everything and/or the updated version published in "
            },
            {
              "type": "link",
              "text": "Monsters of the Multiverse",
              "href": "https://www.dndbeyond.com/sources/motm/fantastical-races"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": ". Because the race changed so little between those two versions, I have decided to combine these two sections. If you’re not using those rules, scroll down to the “Classic Rules” section."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-artificer",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Artificer",
      "sourceAnchor": "artificer",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-artificer-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The Tortle brings nothing to the Artificer with the possible exception of Shell Defense, and that’s simply not enough to make a tortle artificer a good idea. The Tortle’s signature trait is their AC, and artificers will make the Tortle’s natural armor obsolete almost immediately."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-barbarian",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Barbarian",
      "sourceAnchor": "barbarian",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-barbarian-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "17 AC will match half-plate, and realistically even the best-built barbarians\n\tcan’t get their AC above 17 (without magic or a shield, both of which you can\n\talso use) until something like 16th level when they get their 4th ability\n\tscore increase, assuming that they maxed both Strength and Constitution before\n\tmoving on to Dexterity. Even then, most players will go for feats at that\n\tpoint. Without the need to invest in Dexterity, you can put those points into\n\tother ability scores. Dexterity saves are still a problem, but Danger Sense\n\tmitigates them a bit, so even that isn’t a huge problem."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-bard",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Bard",
      "sourceAnchor": "bard",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-bard-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The Tortle’s natural armor helps address martial bards’ MAD problems, but\n\talso allows caster bards to neglect Dexterity in favor of Charisma and feats.\n\tThe additional skill is also nice for a skill-focused character."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-cleric",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Cleric",
      "sourceAnchor": "cleric",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-cleric-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Natural Armor removes the need for clerics in light or medium armor to invest\n\tin Dexterity. That allows you to focus on Constitution and Wisdom and leaves\n\troom for feats."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-druid",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Druid",
      "sourceAnchor": "druid",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-druid-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The Druid’s largest problem is their lack of durability, and the Tortle’s\n\tnatural armor does a lot to address that. The Druid’s best AC options provide\n\t12+ AC so matching the Tortle’s flat 17 AC is exceptionally difficult."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-druid-2",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The Tortle’s AC is such an effective choice for the Druid that I used the Tortle in my example build for the "
            },
            {
              "type": "link",
              "text": "Circle of Spores Handbook",
              "href": "https://rpgbot.net/dnd5/characters/classes/druid/circle-of-spores.html"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": ". The updated version published in Mordenkainen’s Monsters of the Multiverse also allows three +1 increases, allowing you to start with 16 in all three of Dexterity, Constitution, and Wisdom."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-fighter",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Fighter",
      "sourceAnchor": "fighter",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-fighter-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The only interesting thing that the Tortle brings to the Fighter is a skill proficiency."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-monk",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Monk",
      "sourceAnchor": "monk",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-monk-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The Tortle is the best option for Strength-based monks. Unfortunately, the\n\ttortle’s fixed AC won’t keep up with other monks, so your AC will fall behind\n\tby level 8 when most monks will hit AC 18 with Unarmored Defense. You can’t\n\tuse a shield without losing class features, so you’ll lag behind other builds\n\tand never catch up. Still, until level 8 you’ll do just fine."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-paladin",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Paladin",
      "sourceAnchor": "paladin",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-paladin-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The only thing that the Tortle brings to the Paladin is a skill proficiency."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-ranger",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Ranger",
      "sourceAnchor": "ranger",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-ranger-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The Tortle is a great option for a Strength-based ranger since you don’t need\n\thigh Dexterity to max out your AC, and the additional skill proficiency will\n\thelp close the skill gap between the Ranger and the Rogue."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-rogue",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Rogue",
      "sourceAnchor": "rogue",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-rogue-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "17 AC will match the maximum possible AC in light armor right from level 1, and if you were planning to fit feats into your build you might never go to 20 Dexterity. An extra skill proficiency is nice, too."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-sorcerer",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Sorcerer",
      "sourceAnchor": "sorcerer",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-sorcerer-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "17 AC with 8 Dexterity on a class with no armor proficiency."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-warlock",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Warlock",
      "sourceAnchor": "warlock",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-warlock-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Warlocks do get light armor proficiency, so they’re less frail than the Sorcerer or the Wizard, but the Tortle’s natural armor still removes the need for high Dexterity, allowing you to invest resources elsewhere without worrying about your AC."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-wizard",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Wizard",
      "sourceAnchor": "wizard",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-wizard-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "17 AC with 8 Dexterity on a class with no armor proficiency."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Tortle Classes (Classic Rules)",
      "sourceAnchor": "classes-default",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classes-default-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "This section assumes that you’re "
            },
            {
              "type": "emphasis",
              "text": "not"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " using the option “Customizing Your Origin” rules presented in Tasha’s Cauldron of Everything or the updated version of the race published in Mordenkainen’s Monsters of the Multiverse. If you are using those rules, scroll up to the previous section."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classic-artificer",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Artificer",
      "sourceAnchor": "classic-artificer",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classic-artificer-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "No Intelligence increase, and natural armor is wasted on a class famous for\n\tstacking their AC unbelievably high."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classic-barbarian",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Barbarian",
      "sourceAnchor": "classic-barbarian",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classic-barbarian-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "17 AC will match half-plate, and realistically even the best-built barbarians\n\tcan’t get their AC above 17 (without magic or a shield, both of which you can\n\talso use) until something like 16th level when they get their 4th ability\n\tscore increase, assuming that they maxed both Strength and Constitution before\n\tmoving on to Dexterity. Even then, most players will go for feats at that\n\tpoint. Without the need to invest in Dexterity, you can put those points into\n\tother ability scores. Dexterity saves are still a problem, but Danger Sense\n\tmitigates them a bit, so even that isn’t a huge problem."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classic-bard",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Bard",
      "sourceAnchor": "classic-bard",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classic-bard-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "If any race worked for a Strength-based bard, it’s the Tortle. But without a\n\tCharisma increase, the Bard isn’t a great option for tortle spellcasters."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classic-cleric",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Cleric",
      "sourceAnchor": "classic-cleric",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classic-cleric-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Strength and Wisdom are great for front-line clerics, but most such clerics\n\talso get heavy armor proficiency. Technically nothing is preventing you from\n\twearing full plate, but it feels weird to disregard the Tortle’s most unique\n\tracial trait."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classic-cleric-2",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "For clerics in light or medium armor, the Tortle’s natural armor allows you\n\tto dump AC similar to a cleric in heavy armor and invest those resources\n\telsewhere."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classic-druid",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Druid",
      "sourceAnchor": "classic-druid",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classic-druid-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The Druid’s largest problem is their lack of durability, and the Tortle’s\n\tnatural armor does a lot to address that. The Druid’s best AC options provide\n\t12+ AC so matching the Tortle’s flat 17 AC is exceptionally difficult.\n\tStrength is wasted for the most part, but that shouldn’t deter you."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classic-druid-2",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The Tortle’s AC is such an effective choice for the Druid that I used the\n\tTortle in my example build for the\n\t"
            },
            {
              "type": "link",
              "text": "Circle of Spores Handbook",
              "href": "https://rpgbot.net/dnd5/characters/classes/druid/circle-of-spores.html"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classic-fighter",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Fighter",
      "sourceAnchor": "classic-fighter",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classic-fighter-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "You can technically wear full plate, but that feels like a waste of the\n\ttortle’s natural armor. There just isn’t a fighter build that makes good use\n\tof the Tortle’s traits. Literally any race with the same ability score\n\tincreases (lizardolk, wood elf, etc.) will make a more effective fighter."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classic-monk",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Monk",
      "sourceAnchor": "classic-monk",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classic-monk-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The Tortle is the best option for Strength-based monks. Unfortunately, the\n\ttortle’s fixed AC won’t keep up with other monks, so your AC will fall behind\n\tby level 8 when most monks will hit AC 18 with Unarmored Defense. You can’t\n\tuse a shield without losing class features, so you’ll lag behind other builds\n\tand never catch up. Still, until level 8 you’ll do just fine."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classic-paladin",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Paladin",
      "sourceAnchor": "classic-paladin",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classic-paladin-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The ability scores don’t line up very well, and the Paladin has very little\n\tuse for the Tortle’s racial traits."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classic-ranger",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Ranger",
      "sourceAnchor": "classic-ranger",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classic-ranger-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The Tortle is a great option for a Strength-based ranger since you don’t need\n\thigh Dexterity to max out your AC, and the additional skill proficiency will\n\thelp close the skill gap between the Ranger and the Rogue."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classic-rogue",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Rogue",
      "sourceAnchor": "classic-rogue",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classic-rogue-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "No Dexterity increase, and nothing else about the Tortle appeals to the\n\tRogue."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classic-sorcerer",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Sorcerer",
      "sourceAnchor": "classic-sorcerer",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classic-sorcerer-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "No Charisma increase. The Tortle’s high base AC is a fun novelty, but that’s\n\tnot enough. If you want a tortle spellcaster, go for something\n\tWisdom-based."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classic-warlock",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Warlock",
      "sourceAnchor": "classic-warlock",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classic-warlock-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "No Charisma increase. The Tortle’s high base AC is a fun novelty, but that’s\n\tnot enough. If you want a tortle spellcaster, go for something\n\tWisdom-based."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classic-wizard",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-default",
      "heading": "Wizard",
      "sourceAnchor": "classic-wizard",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classic-wizard-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "No Intelligence increase. The Tortle’s high base AC is a fun novelty, but\n\tthat’s not enough. If you want a tortle spellcaster, go for something\n\tWisdom-based."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
